  "answer_text": "The 21st century has seen the birth, codification, and explosion of several brand-new literary genres. These new genres have largely been driven by three major forces: **the rise of digital and social media platforms**, **growing climate and ecological anxiety**, and **a cultural craving for gamification and escapism**. \n\n---\n\n### 1. Digital and Social-Media Native Genres\nThese genres do not merely exist online; their entire structures, lengths, and aesthetics are dictated by the digital platforms on which they were born.\n\n*   **Instapoetry (Early 2010s):** A portmanteau of \"Instagram\" and \"poetry,\" this genre emerged as writers began sharing short, highly visual, minimalist poems on social media. Instapoetry bypasses traditional publishing gatekeepers, featuring simple language, raw emotional themes (like healing, trauma, and self-love), and distinct visual formatting\u2014often accompanied by simple line drawings. Poets like Rupi Kaur and Lang Leav popularized the genre, turning it into a massive commercial force.\n*   **Twitterature / Twitter Fiction (Late 2000s):** Born out of the character limits of Twitter (now X), this genre consists of micro-fiction, aphorisms, or serialized novels told entirely through tweets. Writers use the constraint of 140 (later 280) characters to build suspense and master brevity, often involving real-time reader interaction.\n*   **Cell Phone Novels (*Keitai Shosetsu*) (Early 2000s):** Though its earliest roots trace to the very late 1990s, this genre became a massive cultural and literary phenomenon in Japan in the mid-2000s. These are novels written on and for mobile phones. Because they were originally typed on keypad phones, they are characterized by extremely short chapters, heavy use of dialogue, simple sentences, and poetic spacing. Many of these went on to become physical bestsellers and film adaptations.\n\n---\n\n### 2. Anthropocene & Eco-Fiction\nAs the reality of the climate crisis has intensified, literature has adapted to process the collective anxiety of a changing planet, giving rise to two distinct ecological genres.\n\n*   **Cli-Fi (Climate Fiction - Coined in 2007):** While stories about environmental disaster existed previously, the term \"cli-fi\" was coined by blogger Dan Bloom in 2007 and grew into a formally recognized academic and commercial genre in the 2010s. Unlike traditional science fiction, which might feature distant alien worlds, cli-fi is grounded specifically on Earth, focusing on the immediate psychological, social, and emotional realities of humanity grappling with anthropocentric climate change. \n*   **Solarpunk (Coined in 2008):** Born on the internet in the late 2000s and formalized in a 2012 Brazilian anthology, Solarpunk is a direct rebellion against the corporate nihilism of *Cyberpunk* and the ecological doom of typical post-apocalyptic fiction. It is an optimistic, eco-speculative genre that envisions a sustainable, decentralized, green future where humanity lives in harmony with nature, powered by renewable energy. \n\n---\n\n### 3. Gamified & Escapist Fiction\nIn the 2010s and 2020s, readers began looking for new ways to engage with fiction\u2014either by blending it with interactive gaming structures or by leaning into comforting, low-stakes worlds.\n\n*   **LitRPG (Literary Role-Playing Game - Codified in 2013):** While \"portal fantasies\" (characters entering game worlds) have existed for decades, LitRPG was officially named and codified as a distinct genre in 2013 by the Russian publishing house EKSMO. It explicitly blends novel-length narrative with the mechanics of Role-Playing Games (RPGs). In a LitRPG, game statistics, experience points, quest notifications, and level-ups are literal, visible parts of the text that the characters actively monitor and use to progress. It has exploded in popularity on self-publishing and serialization platforms like Royal Road and Kindle Unlimited.\n*   **Cozy Fantasy (Early 2020s):** For decades, high fantasy was dominated by epic, world-ending stakes, gritty realism, and \"grimdark\" violence. In the early 2020s\u2014greatly accelerated by a collective desire for escapism during the COVID-19 pandemic\u2014**Cozy Fantasy** was codified as a distinct genre. It trades world-ending wars for low-stakes, slice-of-life narratives set in magical worlds. The plots focus on community, quiet domesticity, gentle magic, and deep personal healing (perfectly encapsulated by Travis Baldree's 2022 hit *Legends & Lattes*, which follows an orc opening a coffee shop).",
